    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search only covers the past seven days, so earlier misuse outside that window cannot be excluded.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The burst (new fingerprint, residential proxy, webhook, payout change) happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes, so the evidence predated the scoring. This assumes the check had access to request and account-change data, which the case does not state.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link, but it could equally be a shared processor, a mule account or a common service provider.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** An account owner would usually notice diverted payouts within 30 days, so silence is some evidence. It is weak because reports can lag and no investigation appears to have been run.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new access path minutes after publication, combined with payout redirection and acceleration, is a classic takeover pattern.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** With equal denominators of 100, D's recorded rate is 0 percent against 2 percent for the others. The difference is small enough to be noise, but the recorded rate is lower.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined figure is an average; unless all four accounts sit at exactly 5 percent, at least one is above it.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. It cannot rule out accounts held under another identity or a nominee.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; they may have looked at the same evidence, or the second may have read the first note.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** The payment was blocked, so it could never have produced a chargeback; the missing chargeback says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared templates, a shared descriptor pattern and common settlement accounts are enough to justify looking at the four together, though not enough to conclude they are one operation.\n\nRESULT CF-072 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Yes | A3: No, not by itself | A4: Yes, weakly | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No | B3: Yes | B4: Cannot tell | B5: No | B6: Yes"
